Java is strictly pass-by-value, but the confusion arises because what is passed as a value differs 
between primitives and object references.



What does "pass-by-value" mean?

When a variable is passed to a method, Java passes a copy of the variable's value (i.e., the contents of the variable).

For primitives (like int, double, boolean, etc.): the actual value is passed.

For objects: the value of the reference (i.e., memory address pointing to the object) is passed — not the object itself.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

public class PassByValueExample {
    public static void modifyPrimitive(int num) {
        num = 100;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 50;
        modifyPrimitive(number);
        System.out.println(number); // Output: 50
    }
}


number remains 50 because num received a copy of its value. Changing num does not affect number.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


class Person {
    String name;
}

public class PassByValueExample {
    public static void changeName(Person p) {
        p.name = "Alice";      // Modifies the object referred to
        p = new Person();      // Reassigns the reference (has no effect outside) -- here, these changes are happening on stack m/y
        p.name = "Bob";			// therefore, whatever changes we are doing after creating a new instance, will be happening here only.
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person person = new Person();
        person.name = "John";

        changeName(person);

        System.out.println(person.name); // Output: Alice
    }
}

--------------------Explanation--------
Why is the output "Alice", not "Bob"?


Java passed a copy of the reference to the changeName method.

Inside the method:

p.name = "Alice" changes the object that the original reference also points to.

p = new Person() changes the local copy of the reference, not the original reference.

So, person.name outside the method remains affected only by "Alice".



